    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p52-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,6)$ and $(8,10)$.",
          "worked": "From the line, $y=\\frac{x}{2}+6$. Substitute into the circle:\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^2+\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+6\\right)^2-10x-12\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+6\\right)+36&=0\\\\\n\\frac54x^2-10x&=0\\\\\n5x(x-8)&=0\\\\\nx&=0\\text{ or }8.\n\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=0$, $y=6$. When $x=8$, $y=10$.\n\nThus the intersections are $(0,6)$ and $(8,10)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p52-q11-b-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Find the equation of the perpendicular bisector of ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "AB",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p52-q11-b-definition",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The perpendicular bisector of AB is the perpendicular line passing through the midpoint of AB.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=-2x+16$.",
          "worked": "Midpoint of $AB$:\n$$\\left(\\frac{0+8}{2},\\frac{6+10}{2}\\right)=(4,8).$$\nGradient of $AB$:\n$$m_{AB}=\\frac{10-6}{8-0}=\\frac12.$$\nThe perpendicular bisector has gradient $-2$ and passes through $(4,8)$. Using $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\ny-8&=-2(x-4)\\\\\ny&=-2x+16.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The perpendicular bisector of $AB$ intersects the circle at the points $P$ and $Q$.\n\nFind the exact coordinates of $P$ and $Q$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(5+\\sqrt5,\\,6-2\\sqrt5)$ and $(5-\\sqrt5,\\,6+2\\sqrt5)$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $y=-2x+16$ into the circle:\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^2+(-2x+16)^2-10x-12(-2x+16)+36&=0\\\\\n5x^2-50x+100&=0\\\\\nx^2-10x+20&=0\\\\\n(x-5)^2&=5\\\\\nx&=5\\pm\\sqrt5.\n\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $x=5+\\sqrt5$,\n$$y=-2(5+\\sqrt5)+16=6-2\\sqrt5.$$\nWhen $x=5-\\sqrt5$,\n$$y=-2(5-\\sqrt5)+16=6+2\\sqrt5.$$\nThus the intersections are $(5+\\sqrt5,6-2\\sqrt5)$ and $(5-\\sqrt5,6+2\\sqrt5)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-q11-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Explain why $APBQ$ is a kite and hence find its area.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$PA=PB$ and $QA=QB$, since $P$ and $Q$ lie on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, so $APBQ$ is a kite. Its area is $20\\sqrt5$ square units.",
          "worked": "Both $P$ and $Q$ lie on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, so $PA=PB$ and $QA=QB$. Hence $APBQ$ has two pairs of equal adjacent sides and is a kite. Its diagonals $AB$ and $PQ$ meet at right angles.\n\nUsing the distance formula:\n$$\\begin{align*}\nAB&=\\sqrt{(8-0)^2+(10-6)^2}\\\\\nAB&=4\\sqrt5,\\\\\nPQ&=\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt5)^2+(-4\\sqrt5)^2}\\\\\nPQ&=10.\n\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Area}&=\\frac12\\times AB\\times PQ\\\\\n\\text{Area}&=\\frac12\\times4\\sqrt5\\times10\\\\\n\\text{Area}&=20\\sqrt5\\text{ square units}.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      }
    ],
    "diagramPlacement": "right-of-prompt"
  },
